WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.600
QLoRA.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:05.167
This is the technique that democratized fine-tuning.

3
00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:08.433
Let's see how it fits a big model into a small card.

4
00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:12.833
QLoRA is LoRA stacked on a four bit frozen base.

5
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:17.452
The frozen weights don't need gradients, so they don't need precision either.

6
00:00:17.452 --> 00:00:18.967
We store them in four bits.

7
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:23.200
Small LoRA adapters in bf16 are the only thing trained.

8
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.800
Paged optimizers spill their state to the CPU when memory spikes.

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.033
Here's what happens inside each layer.

10
00:00:31.333 --> 00:00:36.567
Weights are stored in NF4, a four bit format shaped for normally distributed weights.

11
00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:40.500
For each matrix multiply, they are dequantized on the fly.

12
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.667
The math runs in bf16.

13
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:47.033
Gradients flow through into the adapters, and only the adapters change.

14
00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:49.767
Why does this matter so much?

15
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:55.667
Before QLoRA, adapting a seven billion parameter model meant a multi-GPU rig.

16
00:00:55.967 --> 00:00:59.500
Now a single consumer card is enough, even a free Colab.

17
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:03.700
A seven billion model fits where only a one billion model used to.

18
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.100
Here it is in code.

19
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.000
A config asks for four bit NF4 weights with bf16 compute.

20
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:14.333
We load a seven billion parameter Mistral model through it.

21
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:16.583
Then we attach LoRA adapters.

22
00:01:16.583 --> 00:01:18.667
That trains on a free Colab T4.

23
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:22.200
Here's a project you can finish this weekend.

24
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.300
NASA logs are full of abbreviations that generic models expand wrongly.

25
00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:32.400
You QLoRA a Mistral on two thousand log line pairs, on a free Colab T4.

26
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:39.100
You end with a fine-tuned seven billion parameter model on a zero dollar budget, and the adapter to prove it.

27
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.177
Let's recap.

28
00:01:41.177 --> 00:01:44.100
QLoRA is LoRA on a four bit frozen base.

29
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.533
Weights in NF4, adapters in bf16.

30
00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:52.233
Double quantization and paged optimizers save even more memory.

31
00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:57.591
So a seven billion parameter fine-tune fits on a consumer GPU.

32
00:01:57.591 --> 00:01:58.733
Next up: PEFT.
